<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gero the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 900–<a href="/years/965">965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Margrave Gero</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gero the Great was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> noble whom Otto I appointed in 937 to command the eastern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. He extended German rule over the Slavic lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and Oder through harsh campaigns and in 963 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I of Poland</a> to pay tribute. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gernrode-abbey">Gernrode Abbey</a>, and after his death in 965 his vast march was divided.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gero-the-great.jpg" alt="Gero the Great" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Mhf77 at German Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gero.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gero was the son of Count Thietmar, tutor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>. In 937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> is thought to have made him count and margrave on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> in succession to his brother Siegfried. The appointment angered Thankmar, the king's half-brother, who rebelled in 938 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-of-franconia">Eberhard of Franconia</a> and Wichmann the Elder; Thankmar died within a year and Gero kept his march. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg">Thietmar of Merseburg</a> later called him Margrave of the East, and historians long spoke of a March of Gero, though some charters describing it are now considered forgeries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His early war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> went badly, as they refused tribute. After an Obodrite attack in 939, Gero invited thirty Slav chieftains to a banquet and killed all but one. When the Stodorani revolted he restored German control, and he bribed the baptised prince Tugumir to deliver his people; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obodrites</a> and Wilzes then submitted. In 954 he put down a revolt of the Ukrani with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-the-red">Conrad the Red</a>, and in 955 he fought with the king and Liudolf, Duke of Swabia, against a Slav alliance that was defeated on the Drosa.</p><p>While Otto was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 962–963, Gero forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> of the Polans to pay tribute and recognise German sovereignty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a> wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> was reduced to the last degree of servitude. Gero subjected the Liutizi and Milzini and extended German rule over the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bobr">Bober</a>, where the Slav population was reduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the death of his eldest son Siegfried, Otto's godson, Gero went on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 959 and in 960 founded the collegiate church of St Cyriakus and the convent of Gernrode, which received much of his wealth. He died on 20 May 965, when his march reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a>. Unpopular with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobility for his low birth and moral strictness, he was remembered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a> as the marcgrâve Gêre. His territory was split into several marches, among them the Northern March, the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> and the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>, and his tomb at Gernrode bears a painting of about 1350 showing him over a vanquished Wend.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Otto I the Saxon frontier became a system of marches: Hermann Billung commanded on the lower Elbe and <b>Margrave Gero</b> in the middle Elbe lands, imposing tribute on the Slavs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 937 Otto I appointed <b>Gero</b> as margrave on the eastern frontier of Saxony, over the Slavic lands between the Saale and the Elbe and beyond.</blockquote><a href="/state/marca-geronis">Marca Geronis</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Gero</b> died in 965, Otto I made the Saxon Eastern March, later called the March of Lusatia, a separate margraviate.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-lower-lusatia">Margraviate of Lower Lusatia</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of <b>Margrave Gero</b> in 965, Otto I created a march around the stronghold of Meissen, founded by Henry the Fowler in 929 among the Sorbs.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-meissen">Margraviate of Meissen</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Margrave Gero</b> died in 965 his huge march was split, and the northern part, between the Elbe and the Oder north of the Havel, became the Northern March.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-march">Northern March</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Gernrode, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.72,11.14&amp;year=942" aria-label="Gernrode on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4802 375 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Gero the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5252 645h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5252 645h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.72,11.14&amp;year=942">What was here in 942 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
